<p><strong>To reduce missed appointments, make every booking a confirmed, recorded slot instead of a phone promise; give clients a booking number; keep the schedule accurate so staff are not double-booked; and follow up with customers who stop coming. Booking software makes each of these routine.</strong></p>
<p>An empty chair is the most expensive thing in a salon or spa. The therapist is paid, the room is lit and the rent is the same, but no revenue arrives. In Nepal, where many appointments are still agreed over a phone call or a Viber message, missed appointments and last-minute cancellations are common and rarely measured.</p>
<h2>Where missed appointments really come from</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The booking was never properly recorded.</strong> A time agreed on a call and scribbled on the back of a slip is easy to lose.</li>
<li><strong>No confirmation.</strong> The client has nothing to look at, so the slot slips their mind.</li>
<li><strong>Double-bookings.</strong> The salon cancels or reshuffles, and the client does not come back.</li>
<li><strong>No follow-up.</strong> Regulars drift away and nobody notices.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Five fixes that work</h2>
<h3>1. Take bookings in one place</h3>
<p>When bookings come from calls, walk-ins, WhatsApp and Instagram, someone has to copy them into the diary. Every copy is a chance to lose one. Send clients to one <a href="/features/booking/">online booking page</a> and let the system lock the slot. See <a href="/blog/stop-taking-bookings-on-whatsapp-nepal/">why WhatsApp bookings go wrong</a>.</p>
<h3>2. Confirm instantly</h3>
<p>A booking number and an immediate confirmation give the client something to keep. It also gives your staff something to quote when they call to check.</p>
<h3>3. Never double-book</h3>
<p>If two clients arrive for one therapist, one of them is unhappy. Slot locking across staff, room and time removes the problem at the source.</p>
<h3>4. Bring lapsed customers back</h3>
<p>A client who has not visited for 60 or 90 days is not a no-show yet, but is about to become one. <a href="/features/outreach/">Outreach</a> emails customers whose last completed booking is older than your chosen number of days, using templates your team writes, with an optional approval queue so nothing is sent without a check.</p>
<h3>5. Record who actually came</h3>
<p>Mark bookings completed when the service is done. You then know your real attendance rate per branch, per staff member and per service, and you can fix the weak spots instead of guessing. The <a href="/features/reports/">reports</a> give you the daily and monthly picture.</p>
<h2>Deposits for high-value services</h2>
<p>For long sessions such as bridal makeup, tattoo work or multi-hour spa packages, a deposit protects the slot. Whatever you decide, record it against the booking. Read how studios handle this in <a href="/blog/tattoo-studio-booking-and-deposits/">tattoo studio booking and deposits</a>.</p>
<h2>Measure it</h2>
<p>Pick a baseline week. Count bookings made, bookings completed and bookings missed. Run the new process for a month and compare. Even a small drop in empty slots pays for the effort quickly.</p>
